# The server's front door. Caddy is the ONE program on this machine that
# listens on ports 80 and 443; every app binds only to 127.0.0.1 on its
# own private port, and Caddy routes each request to the right one by the
# hostname it was addressed to. Hosting another app later means adding
# another block like the one below - never a second web server.
#
# Caddy also fetches and renews the HTTPS certificate for every hostname
# named in this file, automatically, via Let's Encrypt. That only works
# once the hostname's DNS record points at this server.
#
# Install: copy over /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, then: systemctl reload caddy

# flock-off API. Replace the hostname with your real one. The backend
# listens only on localhost:8000, so this proxy is the only way in.
api.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}
